  "worked_solution": "**Step 1**: Let us call $x$ and $y$ the dimensions of the rectangle. \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14067869/assets/a80e6f5620ba4e77487b675390fa04af778e538ecedbd55c783194cf3ab5772b.png)\n\n**Step 2**: We need to find the *minimum* value of the perimeter\n\n$$\nP=2(x+y).\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: The constraint is that the area is exactly $12.$ This gives the equation\n\n$$\nxy=12.\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Solving the above constraint equation for $y$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\frac{12}{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute the above into our expression for $P$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(x) & =2(x+\\frac{12}{x}) \\\\  & =2x+\\frac{24}{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 5**: We solve the equation ${P}^{\u2032}(x)=0.$ Taking the derivative, we have\n\n$$\n{P}^{\u2032}(x)=2-\\frac{24}{{x}^{2}},\n$$\n\nand solving for the stationary points, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}^{\u2032}(x) & =0 \\\\ 2-\\frac{24}{{x}^{2}} & =0 \\\\ 2{x}^{2} & =24 \\\\ {x}^{2} & =12 \\\\ x & =2\\sqrt{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 6**: We confirm that $x=2\\sqrt{3}$ is a minimum using the second derivative test. The second derivative is\n\n$$\n{P}^{\u2033}(x)=\\frac{48}{{x}^{3}}\\,\u27f9\\,{P}^{\u2033}(2\\sqrt{3})=\\frac{48}{(2\\sqrt{3})^{3}}=\\frac{2\\sqrt{3}}{3}>0\\,.\n$$\n\nSince ${P}^{\u2033}(2\\sqrt{3})$ is positive, we conclude that $x=2\\sqrt{3}$ is indeed a local minimum of the function $P(x).$\n\nFinally, when $x=2\\sqrt{3},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\frac{12}{2\\sqrt{3}}=2\\sqrt{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the dimensions of the rectangle with the smallest perimeter are $x=2\\sqrt{3}$ and $y=2\\sqrt{3}.$ In other words, the rectangle with the smallest perimeter is a square with sides of length $2\\sqrt{3}.$\n\nFinally, the smallest possible perimeter is\n\n$$\nP=4\\cdot 2\\sqrt{3}\u224813.86\n$$\n\nrounded to two decimal places.",
